ID: B2-Ch03a-Intro
TEXT:

Illuna's contact is waiting in a stone cavern alcove not far from the troll ambush. He leans his weight against a carved wooden staff, surrounded by four dead trolls sprawled across the gravel, and tracks our approach with narrow, measuring eyes.

He stands under four feet tall, skin the color of damp lichen, with a hooked nose, oversized pointed ears, and prominent ivory tusks crowding his lower jaw.

A goblin. Knowing Illuna is an ancient spirit sharing a child's frame, having monstrous contacts in Varathia should not come as a shock. Even so, most goblins I read about die before twenty—scavengers forced into gutter-theft and smuggling, hunted down by city garrisons or starved out in the wilderness. This one carries deep creases carved into his leathery green skin, a calm, predatory slouch, and the calculating grin of a loan shark sizing up collateral. A goblin living past two centuries on this island did not manage it through good luck.

"Is this the lessathi you mentioned, Illuna?" the goblin asks in clean Common, fixing his gaze on me.

"Yes," Illuna says.

"And the girl?" The goblin tilts his chin toward Leila. "Another of your traveling companions?"

"An arena division-mate," Illuna replies. "I met her minutes ago."

"Hmm." The goblin runs a claw along the grain of his staff. "A gladiator. Then she has nothing to do with Meridith's faction. That spares us complications."

"Wait," I break in. "You can tell she's lessathi just by looking?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Intro2
TEXT:

"If your magical sense is sharp enough, you can distinguish human and lessathi auras with minimal effort," Illuna says, eyes fixed forward. "When you have spent decades dealing with them, it becomes second nature."

"Can these two be trusted?" the goblin asks her.

Illuna gives me a brief, evaluating glance.

"Barry is trustworthy enough," she says. "I know little about the girl, but she has no reason to oppose our objective. She is a gladiator fighting for survival."

"Hold on," I say, stepping between them. "If we are discussing trust, what about him? How much do you trust this guy? He has the look of a dice-sharp working the alley corners of Bagelberry."

The goblin barks a sharp laugh.

"I like him," the goblin says. "No polished manners, no flattery. Straight to the point."

"To answer your question, Barry," Illuna says evenly, "I do not trust him at all. Nor does he trust me. But we both understand that betrayal carries no profit. His intelligence on monster factions is invaluable, and in exchange, I occasionally eliminate his rivals or assist in covert incursions like this one."

"And what exactly is this covert incursion?" I ask.

"There is a minotaur held in the deep pens," the goblin says. "The arena's undefeated champion. You are slated to face him in the second round, assuming you survive the first. I intend to make him an offer."

"What offer?"

"His freedom, in exchange for swearing his blade to my army."

"Your army?" I look at the four-foot goblin leaning on his staff. "I assumed you were an underground fence trading rumors to banshees."

The goblin chuckles again, unoffended.

"Your disrespect has no bottom, does it?"

"You are speaking to Fyron," Illuna says in a dry, flat voice. "General of all free goblins across Varathia not sworn to Tyrath."

"Tyrath?" I ask. "The red dragon from the eastern ridge?"

"The very same," Illuna says.

"Fascinating," Fyron says, stroking the leathery skin of his chin as he studies me. "Few mortals face Tyrath and live to talk about it. How exactly did you survive his wrath?"

choice(""Who said that I escaped?"", B2-Ch03a-Escaped, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Escaped)
choice(""The ones who fought Tyrath before sound like a bunch of weaklings."", B2-Ch03a-Weaklings, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Weaklings)
choice(""I didn't run. I survived. Your veterans already paid to learn the difference."", B2-Ch03a-Seriously, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Seriously)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Escaped
TEXT:

"Who said that I escaped?"

"Did you talk him into letting you walk?" the goblin general asks. "Or are you claiming you brought down the king of dragons alone?"

"Not alone," Illuna says. "He had intervention from a stillwater you know well."

"Eiden?" Fyron's brow knits.

"Yes. Eiden took an unusual interest in Barry's group. He materialized mid-battle and altered Barry's stat device, granting him access to high-tier magical stats. That gave Barry enough firepower to trade blows with Tyrath directly."

"That makes no sense," Fyron says, voice tightening. "Unless the artifact itself channels the spell, a lessathi cannot cast magic under any circumstances—their auras lack the resonance. Ancient lessathi attempted to integrate magical stats centuries ago, but the research failed. To channel spells through that device, a bearer requires both the suppressed frequency of a lessathi aura and the latent capacity of a human caster. One would need to be both lessathi and a—"

The goblin cuts off abruptly, eyes widening as the pieces slam together. Inside her golden pendant, Arraka bursts into shrill laughter.

"Took you long enough, didn't it?" Arraka cackles.

"You knew?" Fyron demands.

"I put it together when he started slinging fire and lightning against a crypt full of skeletons," Arraka says. "I took him for an ordinary lessathi at first, too. Eiden was probably the only one who spotted it from day one."

"So that is why the stillwater watches him..." Fyron murmurs.

"What are you two talking about?" I ask.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Half, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Half)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Weaklings
TEXT:

"The ones who fought Tyrath before sound like a bunch of weaklings."

"Mind your tongue, lessathi," Fyron says, his grin dropping into hard authority. "Some of my finest veterans died against that beast, and none of them were weaklings. Are you claiming you defeated the dragon king alone?"

"Not alone," Illuna says. "He had intervention from a stillwater you know well."

"Eiden?" Fyron's brow knits.

"Yes. Eiden took an unusual interest in Barry's group. He materialized mid-battle and altered Barry's stat device, granting him access to high-tier magical stats. That gave Barry enough firepower to trade blows with Tyrath directly."

"That makes no sense," Fyron says, voice tightening. "Unless the artifact itself channels the spell, a lessathi cannot cast magic under any circumstances—their auras lack the resonance. Ancient lessathi attempted to integrate magical stats centuries ago, but the research failed. To channel spells through that device, a bearer requires both the suppressed frequency of a lessathi aura and the latent capacity of a human caster. One would need to be both lessathi and a—"

The goblin cuts off abruptly, eyes widening as the pieces slam together. Inside her golden pendant, Arraka bursts into shrill laughter.

"Took you long enough, didn't it?" Arraka cackles.

"You knew?" Fyron demands.

"I put it together when he started slinging fire and lightning against a crypt full of skeletons," Arraka says. "I took him for an ordinary lessathi at first, too. Eiden was probably the only one who spotted it from day one."

"So that is why the stillwater watches him..." Fyron murmurs.

"What are you two talking about?" I ask.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Half, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Half)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Seriously
TEXT:

"I didn't run. I survived. Your veterans already paid to learn the difference."

"I doubt you want an honest answer to that," Fyron says, though his sharp eyes narrow. "Yet your tone carries conviction. Are you claiming you defeated the dragon king alone?"

"Not alone," Illuna says. "He had intervention from a stillwater you know well."

"Eiden?" Fyron's brow knits.

"Yes. Eiden took an unusual interest in Barry's group. He materialized mid-battle and altered Barry's stat device, granting him access to high-tier magical stats. That gave Barry enough firepower to trade blows with Tyrath directly."

"That makes no sense," Fyron says, voice tightening. "Unless the artifact itself channels the spell, a lessathi cannot cast magic under any circumstances—their auras lack the resonance. Ancient lessathi attempted to integrate magical stats centuries ago, but the research failed. To channel spells through that device, a bearer requires both the suppressed frequency of a lessathi aura and the latent capacity of a human caster. One would need to be both lessathi and a—"

The goblin cuts off abruptly, eyes widening as the pieces slam together. Inside her golden pendant, Arraka bursts into shrill laughter.

"Took you long enough, didn't it?" Arraka cackles.

"You knew?" Fyron demands.

"I put it together when he started slinging fire and lightning against a crypt full of skeletons," Arraka says. "I took him for an ordinary lessathi at first, too. Eiden was probably the only one who spotted it from day one."

"So that is why the stillwater watches him..." Fyron murmurs.

"What are you two talking about?" I ask.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Half, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Half)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Half
TEXT:

"They are saying you are half-lessathi," Illuna says calmly. "Half human, half lessathi."

Leila snaps her head toward Illuna, eyes wide, tracking her with sudden, intense focus. It is the sharpest reaction she has shown since we entered the caverns.

"Half-lessathi," I repeat, turning the thought.

"It is the only coherent explanation," Fyron says. "No other bloodline carries that physical structure. And with human lineage mixed in, channeling magical stats becomes theoretically possible."

"Makes little difference to me either way," I say with a shrug. "Label or not, I have no idea how to access those stats on command. It took a massive external energy surge to unlock them for two minutes against Tyrath. That hardly helps in a street fight."

"A permanent override exists," Fyron notes. "Though unlocking it requires cipher keys held exclusively by the highest council of the lessathi order. You will not be activating them anytime soon."

"Fascinating heritage lesson," I say, glancing down the dark rock corridor. "Can we move before the local garrison sends reinforcements to check on their dead?"

"Reinforcements?" Fyron scoffs, gesturing with his staff at the butchered troll corpses around his feet. "I silenced their scout patrol. Nobody knows we are here."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Bodies, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Bodies)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Bodies
TEXT:

"Speaking of rotting carcasses," Arraka chimes from the amulet, "are we hiding the evidence, or stringing them up as warning totems? Personally, I vote for decorative mutilation."

"Can't you wake Flower and have her incinerate them?" I ask Illuna.

"I avoid waking Flower on covert missions," Illuna says flatly. "Discretion is not in her vocabulary, and she and Fyron despise each other."

"Save your energy," Fyron says. "I have it under control."

He drives the heel of his staff into the stone floor. Searing crimson flames erupt across every troll corpse simultaneously, engulfing meat and bone in a white-hot hiss. Within seconds the fire collapses into fine gray ash drifting over the gravel.

"Shall we?" the goblin asks.

"Arraka, any hostiles ahead?" Illuna asks.

"Clear," the banshee grunts. "You slaughtered the perimeter guards."

"No hidden casters cloaked in illusions?"

"Nothing."

Leila raises her hands, blue glowing runes forming in the air before Illuna.

Did you say the spirit in that amulet is named Arraka?

"I did," Illuna says. "You know of her?"

I have... heard accounts, yes...

choice(""What exactly have you heard about her?"", B2-Ch03a-Eyesight, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Eyesight, v_b2_ch3_spin = 1)
choice(""Did the accounts mention the chain? Spin it and she stops being useful for a few seconds."", B2-Ch03a-Eyesight, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Eyesight, v_b2_ch3_spin = 2)
choice(""Spinning the chain nauseates her. If you need proof she is the same Arraka, watch."", B2-Ch03a-Eyesight, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Eyesight, v_b2_ch3_spin = 3)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Eyesight
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch3_spin == 1) {
"What exactly have you heard about her?"

I know she is a powerful, malevolent entity from the Still Winter, Leila writes. Is this genuinely the same Arraka from six hundred years ago?

"It is her," Illuna says. "Without question."

"Who in the hells are you two talking to?" Arraka snaps from the pendant. "Have you both lost your minds?"

"We were talking to Leila," I say.

"Who in the hells is Leila?"

"The lessathi girl," Illuna replies. "She communicates through luminous glyphs written in the air."

"Oh! That explains the floating sparks," Arraka grumbles.

"You couldn't read them?" I ask.

"I don't have eyes, you idiot! I perceive ambient magical residue. To my sense, her drawings were just scattered energy particles drifting in the dark. Now that I know they form glyphs, I can parse them."
}
#if(v_b2_ch3_spin == 2) {
"Did the accounts mention the chain? Spin it and she stops being useful for a few seconds."

No... Leila writes, brow furrowing. The chronicles do not mention that vulnerability...

"Who in the hells are you two talking to?" Arraka snaps from the pendant. "Have you both lost your minds?"

"We were talking to Leila," I say.

"Who in the hells is Leila?"

"The lessathi girl," Illuna replies. "She communicates through luminous glyphs written in the air."

"Oh! That explains the floating sparks," Arraka grumbles.

"You couldn't read them?" I ask.

"I don't have eyes, you idiot! I perceive ambient magical residue. To my sense, her drawings were just scattered energy particles drifting in the dark. Now that I know they form glyphs, I can parse them."
}
#if(v_b2_ch3_spin == 3) {
"Spinning the chain nauseates her. If you need proof she is the same Arraka, watch."

I take the chain from Illuna and whip the amulet into a frantic blur. Illuna lets out a long sigh.

That... is not what she is famous for, Leila writes, staring in bewilderment.

"What in the blazes are you doing?!" Arraka screeches, her voice warbling with the rotation. "Show who? Who are you even talking to? Has dungeon air rotted your tiny brain?!"

"I was talking to Leila," I say, catching the pendant.

"Who in the hells is Leila?" Arraka snarls.

"The lessathi girl," Illuna says, reclaiming the chain and settling the amulet back over her chest. "She communicates through luminous glyphs written in the air."

"Oh! That explains the floating sparks," Arraka grumbles.

"You couldn't read them?" I ask.

"I don't have eyes, you idiot! I perceive ambient magical residue. To my sense, her drawings were just scattered energy particles drifting in the dark. Now that I know they form glyphs, I can parse them."
}
Is it safe carrying her around like that? Leila writes, gaze lingering on the pendant.

"Don't worry, she's all bark," I say. "I fought her with a dagger when she had a full host body, and she still couldn't put me down."

"You love testing your luck, don't you, Barry-boy?" Arraka mutters.

"As long as she stays inside that amulet, she poses no threat," Illuna tells Leila. "Move out. We have lingered in the open too long."

She pauses.

"First, healing. Those lacerations and contusions are hours old. I can mend them without strain. Barry, whether that blood on your tunic is yours or the trolls' I cannot tell, but I will patch you up next if needed."

Thank you, Leila writes as Illuna gathers pale blue mana around her hands, stitching torn skin and easing the deep bruising on Leila's arms.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Accounting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Accounting)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Accounting
TEXT:

With healing finished, Fyron takes point, leading us down an empty tunnel branching opposite our entry route.

"An ingenious method of communication, young lady," Fyron remarks over his shoulder. "Judging by the resonance of those light-particles, I take it you are operating a heavily modified stat device to broadcast your thoughts?"

Leila pauses, meeting the goblin's gaze for several seconds before raising her hand to write.

I see that you are very knowledgeable when it comes to magical devices, Leila writes.

"I have modified a few relics in my time, including this staff," Fyron says. "Nothing on the scale of your translator, though. Reading neural intent and projecting coherent glyphs in real time requires profound expertise in mind-reading matrices. Whoever built it is a master craftsman."

He is, Leila writes, an austere pride settling over her features.

"So," I say, stepping into pace beside Fyron, "why the interest in recruiting a minotaur? Preparing for a campaign against Tyrath?"

"Fascinating," Fyron says, a thin smirk touching his tusks. "You stand to gain nothing from the answer, yet you casually pry into my vital military logistics without offering a shred of collateral. You are clearly no spy—a spy would possess the wit not to ask directly. What prompts the inquiry?"

"What humans call 'small talk,'" Illuna says in her flat monotone. "Vacuous noise generated purely to fill silence. Barry lacks all concept of discretion, so the impropriety never crossed his mind."

"Spare me the lecture," I say. "If you were truly worried about operational secrecy, you wouldn't have brought me along on a minotaur rescue. If you think I'm a security risk, just tell me you need him to balance the camp ledgers and leave it at that. It's not like I can cross-check your records."

"Ah. So you have a brain after all," Fyron chuckles. "A relief. In that case, as you said, the minotaur will join our accounting department upon concluding his orientation. I anticipate exemplary service."

Arraka cackles from the amulet.

"If I were you, I'd put him on ledgers regardless, just for the insult! Minotaurs consider any labor without blood or heavy lifting to be the peak of dishonor."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Tiger, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Tiger)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Tiger
TEXT:

"I will bear that in mind," Fyron says. He taps his staff against a jagged outcrop on the tunnel wall. Stone grinds against stone as a concealed door pivots inward.

"You know these undercrofts well," I say, stepping through the newly revealed corridor.

"Fyron spends half his life unearthing subterranean ruins and forgotten passages," Illuna says. "His pastimes align uncomfortably well with Flower's."

"Watch yourself, Illuna," Fyron counters. "My so-called pastime is the only reason we have moved through this complex without tripping alarms. Do not insult me by comparing my work to that simpleton you host."

"Have you forgotten it was thanks to that simpleton you constructed that staff?" Illuna asks coldly. "We would never have crossed paths in that magma cavern twenty years ago if Flower hadn't fallen for an elementary ruse."

"Aha—hahaha!" Arraka howls. "I still cherish the look on her face when she woke surrounded by fire elementals! Believing my bluff was absolute perfection!"

"Hello?" a rasping voice calls from a dark passage to our right. "Is someone there? Can you hear me?"

We freeze in unison, turning toward the cross-tunnel. The voice drifts from deep in the dark, muffled by distance.

"Please! If anyone is out there, help! I have been locked down here for months. Anything you ask—I swear it! Please!"

"Hmm," Fyron murmurs. He channels a pulse through his staff, launching a floating sphere of pale amber light down the passage.

The glow settles over a reinforced iron cell at the far end, illuminating a single captive: a massive tiger crouched behind thick bars.

"Were you the one calling out?" Fyron asks, walking up to the grate.

"Yes," the tiger answers in deep, fluent Common. "Please, break this lock! I will serve whatever purpose you require!"

"Indeed?" Fyron's smile turns predatory. "Is it wise to address strangers in Common? Rumor has it the golden fox executes any beast caught speaking civilized tongues these days."

"The golden fox can rot in the abyss along with her decrees!" the tiger snarls. "I am never returning to that wretched domain!"

"Abandoning the sacred woods?" Fyron tilts his head. "Where else would a beast find shelter? Who protects you, if not the spirit fox?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Protection, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Protection)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Protection
TEXT:

"I don't need anyone's protection!" the tiger says.

The goblin grins.

"Is that why you sit in a dungeon cell, pleading with passersby for rescue? Because you do not need anyone's protection?"

The tiger lowers his massive head, whiskers twitching in bitter silence.

"Let me reconstruct the sequence," Fyron says. "Judging by your hostility, you deserted the fox's territory long before the arena slavers netted you. Correct?"

"Yes," the tiger mutters, eyes fixed on the floor.

"Were other beasts captured alongside you?"

"No. I have been living by myself ever since I left the sacred woods."

"I see."

Fyron guides the orb of light closer to the iron bars. As the sphere nears the metal, its amber radiance dims and flickers.

"As expected," the goblin notes. "The lattice restricts the use of magic. Am I to assume you are a mage?"

"I am," the tiger says.

Fyron's grin sharpens as he glances back at me.

"Fortunate timing, Barry. I may have discovered the ideal candidate to direct my army's accounting team! What do you think? Does he look fit for the task?"

choice(""I'm going to tell it to you straight. I don't see him holding a pen anytime soon."", B2-Ch03a-Straight, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Straight)
choice(""I see that it didn't take you long to adapt to our human 'small talk'."", B2-Ch03a-Adapt, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Adapt)
choice(""Could we stop calling them accountants? It's beginning to sound confusing."", B2-Ch03a-Confusing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Confusing)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Straight
TEXT:

"I'm going to tell it to you straight. I don't see him holding a pen anytime soon."

"Claws are no impediment to leadership," Fyron replies smoothly. "He only requires a head for numbers."

"Wait," the tiger says, ears flattening. "You want me to do what?..."

The goblin laughs.

"Pay no attention. We were only jesting. Although, you did say that you'd do anything I ask, so sorting ledgers ought to be a welcome assignment."

"I suppose..." the tiger mumbles.

"Illuna, sever these iron bars," Fyron instructs. "The anti-magic field does not spread beyond those bars, so your water whips can shear the iron from the outside."

"Very well," Illuna says.

She extends both hands, twin tendrils of pressurized water forming in her grip. With two swift diagonal lashes, she slices the iron bars into pieces.

"You are free!" Fyron tells the tiger as the beast steps through the severed threshold. "Which is to say, free to do my bidding. I trust you are not planning to betray me and rejoin the fox immediately after we get out of this place."

"I assure you, that is not going to happen," the tiger says.

"Why such fierce resentment toward the fox?" Fyron asks, pointing his staff. "What drove an animal of your standing to refuse to return to your homeland, even after all this time?"

"I have my reasons," the tiger says.

"Vague answers make poor collateral," Fyron warns, eyes narrowing. "What proves this exile story isn't a fabrication to gain my sympathy?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Leopard, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Leopard)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Adapt
TEXT:

"I see that it didn't take you long to adapt to our human 'small talk'."

"That is because your 'small talk' is not too different from what we used to call 'goblin politics' not so long ago," Fyron replies smoothly.

"And they've changed since then?" I ask him.

"No. I simply took over."

"Wait," the tiger says, ears flattening. "What was it you said you wanted me to do, again?..."

The goblin laughs.

"Pay no attention. We were only jesting. Although, you did say that you'd do anything I ask, so sorting ledgers ought to be a welcome assignment."

"I suppose..." the tiger mumbles.

"Illuna, sever these iron bars," Fyron instructs. "The anti-magic field does not spread beyond those bars, so your water whips can shear the iron from the outside."

"Very well," Illuna says.

She extends both hands, twin tendrils of pressurized water forming in her grip. With two swift diagonal lashes, she slices the iron bars into pieces.

"You are free!" Fyron tells the tiger as the beast steps through the severed threshold. "Which is to say, free to do my bidding. I trust you are not planning to betray me and rejoin the fox immediately after we get out of this place."

"I assure you, that is not going to happen," the tiger says.

"Why such fierce resentment toward the fox?" Fyron asks, pointing his staff. "What drove an animal of your standing to refuse to return to your homeland, even after all this time?"

"I have my reasons," the tiger says.

"Vague answers make poor collateral," Fyron warns, eyes narrowing. "What proves this exile story isn't a fabrication to gain my sympathy?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Leopard, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Leopard)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Confusing
TEXT:

"Could we stop calling them accountants? It's beginning to sound confusing."

"A fair point!" Fyron says. "Perhaps accountant isn't the right word to use in this situation. Maybe I should have said that I'm looking to have him work as a consultant, instead?"

"Wait," the tiger says, ears flattening. "You want me to do what?..."

The goblin laughs.

"Pay no attention. We were only jesting. Although, you did say that you'd do anything I ask, so sorting ledgers ought to be a welcome assignment."

"I suppose..." the tiger mumbles.

"Illuna, sever these iron bars," Fyron instructs. "The anti-magic field does not spread beyond those bars, so your water whips can shear the iron from the outside."

"Very well," Illuna says.

She extends both hands, twin tendrils of pressurized water forming in her grip. With two swift diagonal lashes, she slices the iron bars into pieces.

"You are free!" Fyron tells the tiger as the beast steps through the severed threshold. "Which is to say, free to do my bidding. I trust you are not planning to betray me and rejoin the fox immediately after we get out of this place."

"I assure you, that is not going to happen," the tiger says.

"Why such fierce resentment toward the fox?" Fyron asks, pointing his staff. "What drove an animal of your standing to refuse to return to your homeland, even after all this time?"

"I have my reasons," the tiger says.

"Vague answers make poor collateral," Fyron warns, eyes narrowing. "What proves this exile story isn't a fabrication to gain my sympathy?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Leopard, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Leopard)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Leopard
TEXT:

"The fact that I'm speaking to you in the Common language," the tiger says.

"Explain," Fyron says.

"Eleya, the great golden fox," the tiger says, fur bristling along his spine, "has a habit of interrogating all of the animals that return to her domain after a long period of captivity. One question that is always asked is if you'd spoken to any humans in Common while you were away. There is no way to hide the truth from the great fox. As long as she studies your aura, she can easily tell if you are lying. Even if I weren't an exile, going back to my homeland now would be nothing short of suicide. She would execute me on the spot."

"I admit that I have heard of the fox's interrogations," Fyron says. "But I assumed that she would make an exception for mages and other valuable members of her community."

"The fox does not make exceptions," the tiger says, fury and terror warring in his golden eyes. "I used to think the same as you, before she murdered the leopard's son in cold blood."

"The leopard?" I say. "You mean Leo, the leopard?"

"Yes, that is who I was referring to," the tiger says. "He was her most loyal retainer. He still is. But that did not stop her from executing his cub, for the crime of begging for his life in Common to a couple of human hunters. The leopard may have accepted her judgment, but I never will."

He stops, teeth bared, breathing hard.

"I never will," he says.

"Ah," Fyron murmurs, nodding slowly. "Now things make sense. If the fox later executed your family in a similar manner, I can see why you would choose to leave her domain forever. That would also explain why you left your homeland by yourself."

The tiger's muscles bunch with suppressed rage, but he holds his silence.

"You've said enough," Fyron tells the tiger. "At the very least, I find it unlikely that you would return to the golden fox after all you've said. Regarding your debt to me, I ask only that you accompany me to my goblin camp on my way back. If the life there is not to your liking, then you may leave at any point, and consider your debt to me paid. I am, however, confident that you will choose to remain."

"And why is that?" the tiger asks.

"You will understand once you get there," Fyron says. "Now, follow us. There is still another prisoner that we need to rescue before we can leave this place."

"Another prisoner?" the tiger asks as he joins us, and we all resume our walk through the caves. "You don't mean the minotaur, do you?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Animals, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Animals)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Animals
TEXT:

"Who else could I mean?" Fyron says. "Is there anyone else here that would be worth the effort of releasing?"

"I suppose not," the tiger says. "The minotaur is likely the only prisoner from this place aside from me who is not some mindless beast."

"I'm curious," I say. "You say that all the creatures trapped in these caverns are mindless beasts, but I've also seen some sabre-toothed tigers on my way here. Do you feel the same way about them, too?"

"Of course I do," the tiger snaps, giving me a furious look. "What kind of question is this?"

"Well, they are tigers..." I say.

"No, they aren't!" the tiger says. "They are sabre-toothed tigers, not tigers! How can you even compare the two?"

"Barry is not from Varathia," Illuna explains calmly. "Outside of Varathia, there is no difference between animals and creatures. A tiger from the Western Continent is just as much of a mindless beast as a sabre-toothed predator."

"You can't be serious!" the tiger says.

"On the contrary," Illuna says. "I am quite serious. I do not know if the high level of intelligence shown by the animals in Varathia is due to the golden fox's influence or other factors, but Varathia is the only place in the world where animals can speak, and the only place where they live in civilized societies."

"Hah!" Arraka says from the amulet. "How does it feel to know that there are animals out there who are dumber than ogres? Did you think that you were anything special? I'm willing to bet that people from outside of Varathia would not even be able to tell the difference between you and a wild beast. To them, you're nothing but a mindless creature, no different from a harpy or a giant slug. Isn't that right, Barry?"

choice(""Well, it's a bit hard to treat him like a dumb beast, when he can speak Common."", B2-Ch03a-Dumb, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Dumb)
choice(""Yeah, to me, he's no different from a harpy, a giant slug, or a banshee in an amulet."", B2-Ch03a-Amulet, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Amulet)
choice(""Yeah, creatures and animals are all the same to me, to be honest."", B2-Ch03a-Same, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Same)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Dumb
TEXT:

"Well, it's a bit hard to treat him like a dumb beast, when he can speak Common."

"Don't worry, I have an easy fix for that!" Arraka says. "Just ignore what he says. If you don't pay any attention to him, you'll find it much easier to treat him like an animal. Oh, I'm sorry, did I say animal? What I meant to say was creature. I always get the two mixed up! Aha—AHAHAHAHAHA!"

"Who the hell is that clown from the amulet?" the tiger says. "Some sort of prankster spirit?"

"That's rude, you know!" Arraka snaps. "You should not assume I'm a clown just because the girl wearing my amulet is dressed in clown clothes! Don't they teach you manners in animal school?"

"As unbelievable as it may sound," Illuna says, ignoring Arraka, "the banshee trapped in this amulet is the one responsible for the exile of all the banshees to the earthen plane. The gods didn't want to deal with her, so they banished her here, along with all her kind. Or so she claims."

"I can see why they'd want her out of the magical plane," the tiger says. "Even the gods would run out of patience when dealing with such idiocy."

"Oh, yeah?" Arraka says. "Well, I'll have you know that—"

She does not get to finish what she had to say, because Illuna snaps the amulet shut in the middle of her sentence.

"I've heard enough of this," Illuna says. "We have more important matters to discuss right now."

She turns to me.

"Barry, do you and Leila have a means of returning to your cells? Fyron and I will be teleporting out of here as soon as we free the minotaur, so we won't be there to escort you back."

"No, we don't have a means of returning to our cells," I say. "The way we came is sealed shut and a hundred feet above the ground. We need to find another way back."

"If you're looking to reach the main corridors below the arena," Fyron says, "there's a secret tunnel that I can open for you, not far from where the minotaur is located. You might have to get past several traps, in order to reach your destination, however."

"That's... awful nice of you," I say.

"Do not worry," Illuna says, seeing the mistrust on my face. "Fyron is not foolish enough to risk his alliance with me for no other reason than to amuse himself. He is not Arraka. If he says that there's a tunnel leading to the corridors below the arena, then he speaks the truth."

"I see," I say. "Well, in that case, thanks, I guess..."

"There's no need to thank me, Barry," Fyron says with a smirk. "I never help people for free. There may come a time when I will be in need of your help as well. If that time ever comes, I'd rather have you owe me a favor, than the other way around. That's all there is to it."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Maze, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Maze)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Amulet
TEXT:

"Yeah, to me, he's no different from a harpy, a giant slug, or a banshee in an amulet."

"Huh..." Arraka says. "I know that I should be more upset about this, but given the fact that there are many people out there who actually cannot tell the difference between a banshee and a harpy, I'll admit that I walked right into this one. I'd give you a slow clap to congratulate you for your sharp wit, but alas, I have no hands!"

"It's the thought that counts," I say.

"Who the hell is that clown from the amulet?" the tiger says. "Some sort of prankster spirit?"

"That's rude, you know!" Arraka snaps. "You should not assume I'm a clown just because the girl wearing my amulet is dressed in clown clothes! Don't they teach you manners in animal school?"

"As unbelievable as it may sound," Illuna says, ignoring Arraka, "the banshee trapped in this amulet is the one responsible for the exile of all the banshees to the earthen plane. The gods didn't want to deal with her, so they banished her here, along with all her kind. Or so she claims."

"I can see why they'd want her out of the magical plane," the tiger says. "Even the gods would run out of patience when dealing with such idiocy."

"Oh, yeah?" Arraka says. "Well, I'll have you know that—"

She does not get to finish what she had to say, because Illuna snaps the amulet shut in the middle of her sentence.

"I've heard enough of this," Illuna says. "We have more important matters to discuss right now."

She turns to me.

"Barry, do you and Leila have a means of returning to your cells? Fyron and I will be teleporting out of here as soon as we free the minotaur, so we won't be there to escort you back."

"No, we don't have a means of returning to our cells," I say. "The way we came is sealed shut and a hundred feet above the ground. We need to find another way back."

"If you're looking to reach the main corridors below the arena," Fyron says, "there's a secret tunnel that I can open for you, not far from where the minotaur is located. You might have to get past several traps, in order to reach your destination, however."

"That's... awful nice of you," I say.

"Do not worry," Illuna says, seeing the mistrust on my face. "Fyron is not foolish enough to risk his alliance with me for no other reason than to amuse himself. He is not Arraka. If he says that there's a tunnel leading to the corridors below the arena, then he speaks the truth."

"I see," I say. "Well, in that case, thanks, I guess..."

"There's no need to thank me, Barry," Fyron says with a smirk. "I never help people for free. There may come a time when I will be in need of your help as well. If that time ever comes, I'd rather have you owe me a favor, than the other way around. That's all there is to it."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Maze, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Maze)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Same
TEXT:

"Yeah, creatures and animals are all the same to me, to be honest."

"Then you can continue to live happily in your blissful ignorance, human," the tiger says. "I could not care less about your opinion of me."

He looks toward Arraka's amulet.

"And who the hell is that clown from the amulet? Some sort of prankster spirit?"

"That's rude, you know!" Arraka snaps. "You should not assume I'm a clown just because the girl wearing my amulet is dressed in clown clothes! Don't they teach you manners in animal school?"

"As unbelievable as it may sound," Illuna says, ignoring Arraka, "the banshee trapped in this amulet is the one responsible for the exile of all the banshees to the earthen plane. The gods didn't want to deal with her, so they banished her here, along with all her kind. Or so she claims."

"I can see why they'd want her out of the magical plane," the tiger says. "Even the gods would run out of patience when dealing with such idiocy."

"Oh, yeah?" Arraka says. "Well, I'll have you know that—"

She does not get to finish what she had to say, because Illuna snaps the amulet shut in the middle of her sentence.

"I've heard enough of this," Illuna says. "We have more important matters to discuss right now."

She turns to me.

"Barry, do you and Leila have a means of returning to your cells? Fyron and I will be teleporting out of here as soon as we free the minotaur, so we won't be there to escort you back."

"No, we don't have a means of returning to our cells," I say. "The way we came is sealed shut and a hundred feet above the ground. We need to find another way back."

"If you're looking to reach the main corridors below the arena," Fyron says, "there's a secret tunnel that I can open for you, not far from where the minotaur is located. You might have to get past several traps, in order to reach your destination, however."

"That's... awful nice of you," I say.

"Do not worry," Illuna says, seeing the mistrust on my face. "Fyron is not foolish enough to risk his alliance with me for no other reason than to amuse himself. He is not Arraka. If he says that there's a tunnel leading to the corridors below the arena, then he speaks the truth."

"I see," I say. "Well, in that case, thanks, I guess..."

"There's no need to thank me, Barry," Fyron says with a smirk. "I never help people for free. There may come a time when I will be in need of your help as well. If that time ever comes, I'd rather have you owe me a favor, than the other way around. That's all there is to it."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Maze, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Maze)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Maze
TEXT:

"Fyron, is this the last tunnel before the labyrinth?" Illuna asks.

"Yes," Fyron says. "The tunnel's exit should leave us very close to the maze's entrance."

"Wait, what maze?" I say.

"Do you know that old legend," Fyron says, "in which the gods worked together to build an elaborate maze for the sole purpose of trapping the king of the minotaurs? Well, the owner of the arena liked the story so much that he decided to build his own underground labyrinth as soon as he got hold of a minotaur. Unfortunately, I did not have a chance to visit these tunnels since the maze was created, so once we reach it, I won't know my way around as well as I have so far."

"I don't think that will be a problem," Illuna says, "since there will likely be no need to hide ourselves anymore, once we reach the maze. They will surely have caught on to our presence by then. I think we should be able to handle their guards by ourselves easily, but if worst comes to worst, I'll wake up Flower as well."

I'm sorry, who is Flower? Leila writes, confused.

"Flower is the little girl inhabiting this body," Illuna says.

You mean... she's still alive? Leila writes, shocked. But, aren't you a banshee? Didn't you consume her soul when you took over her body?

Illuna lets out a dry chuckle.

"If only it would have been that easy," she says. "Unfortunately, the two of us have been stuck together in this body for the past twenty years, and we'll likely continue living like this for years to come. Our souls are fused."

I see... Leila writes, simply.

She looks as if she has further questions, but decides against asking. As the tunnel's exit draws closer, Illuna reopens Arraka's amulet, asking if she can spot any enemies nearby.

"Oh, so now you want me to talk?" Arraka says. "You're not going to close the amulet in my face anymore? Well, what if I don't want to talk? What do you say to that?"

"If you're not planning to talk, then I have no further need of you," Illuna says, thumb resting on the clasp. "Have a good nap."

"Wait, wait, wait!" Arraka says before the hinge snaps shut. "Fine! You win! There are some trolls much further down the next tunnel, but they're not in viewing range of the maze's entrance. The coast is clear."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Stubborn, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Stubborn)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Stubborn
TEXT:

"That doesn't sound right," Illuna says. "They should have at least some guards at the entrance of the labyrinth. I'm much more inclined to believe that they've become aware of our presence, and that they deliberately emptied the next tunnel, in order to lure us into the maze."

"That changes little," Fyron says. "The only way to reach the minotaur is through the maze. If the trolls choose to stand in our way, then we'll just have to make our way through them."

Once we reach the next corridor, we verify that the way is clear before crossing into the maze. The transition is immediate and strange. The rough, earthy rock of the natural caverns gives way to sheer, pitch-black walls that drink in the light like coal.

Fyron, noticing the difference, steps close to one of the walls. He swipes the surface with his index finger, then brings the blackened residue to his face, inspecting it attentively.

"Tell me, Arraka," the goblin says, "are you sensing any trolls nearby?"

"What?" Arraka says. "No, I already told you that the coast is clear."

"Are you sure?" Fyron asks.

"What kind of question is that?" Arraka snaps. "Of course I'm sure!"

"Oh?" Fyron says. "Then could you perhaps describe to me what lies beyond this black wall?"

"Yeah, hold on a sec," Arraka says.

For about ten seconds, we all wait in silence for Arraka's answer.

"Well?" Fyron says. "Weren't you going to describe to us what's on the other side?"

"There's... a bit of interference," Arraka mutters. "I'm still working on it."

"Oh, then by all means," Fyron purrs, "do not let this old goblin's ramblings interrupt you from your work. Please, take all the time you need."

Thirty more seconds of dead silence pass before Illuna's composure snaps.

"Good gods!" she shouts. "How long is it going to take you to admit the obvious? This wall is clearly made from a material that blocks magical sense. We can wait here all day, and you're still not going to get through it. Are you really that stubborn?"

"Fine!" Arraka shrieks. "I admit it! I can't get through it. I have no idea what's on the other side. Are you happy now?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Seredium, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Seredium)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Seredium
TEXT:

Fyron grins.

"Thank you, Arraka, I believe that will suffice," he says. "As Illuna noted, this wall is made from a material that does not allow magical sense to pass through. This type of black rock is not uncommon in underground caves such as this, and it is called 'seredium'. The owner of the arena must have chosen to build his labyrinth here specifically because of the existence of these rocks. There wouldn't be much point in making a labyrinth if people could just use their magical sense to see through walls, now would there?"

While the goblin speaks, Leila inspects the floor of the cave with intense focus. Fyron notices her, walking closer to see what she is examining so attentively.

"Is there something special about this corridor's floor?" Fyron asks her.

Leila nods. She points toward a dark smear on the stone, nearly indistinguishable from the black floor around it.

This is no ordinary black stain, Leila writes. It is troll blood. Look, there's more over there. And there as well. The trail is fresh. If we follow the drops of blood, they might just lead us to the minotaur.

"Or the trolls could very well be baiting us, so that we walk directly into their ambush!" the tiger says. "Since when do trolls leave blood trails, anyway? Don't they have regeneration powers that heal all of their wounds in a matter of seconds?"

"Not all of them," Illuna says. "The effectiveness of their regeneration varies from individual to individual. There are also certain types of wounds that heal slower than others. We have no way of knowing whether this trail was left deliberately or accidentally. Not that it matters, really. As long as we aim to reach the minotaur, we're going to run into the trolls sooner or later, whether we follow this trail or not."

choice("Propose to follow the blood trail", B2-Ch03a-Follow, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Follow)
choice("Propose to avoid the blood trail", B2-Ch03a-Avoid, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Avoid, v_b2_ch3_troll = 0)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Follow
TEXT:

"In that case, I propose we follow the trail," I say. "If the trolls left it deliberately, then we'll have to fight them, like we were already planning to. But if they did it by accident, then at least we won't have to waste hours of our time navigating the labyrinth."

"A valid proposal," Fyron says. "Does anyone have any objections?"

"As I've said, it's all the same to me," Illuna says.

"Do what you want," the tiger says.

Leila remains silent, offering no objections.

"Very well, then," Fyron says. "We will pursue the blood trail. However, our first concern should be the mapping of this maze. Arraka, I understand that you have the ability to generate holograms?"

"Yeah," Arraka says. "But I can't make a map of this place if my magical sense can't pass through the walls."

"It doesn't matter," Fyron says. "You can just draw the map as we walk. What's important is that we don't lose track of where we are. I will be marking the walls periodically, as we advance through the maze, for that same purpose."

"Alright then," Arraka says. "Here goes."

She conjures a glowing miniature projection, similar to the one from the ogre stronghold, where our positions show as colored dots and the surrounding maze is blanketed in black fog, except for our immediate corridor. Meanwhile, Fyron uses his staff to imprint a complex symbol in glowing green ink onto the right-hand wall.

"We're good to go," Fyron says. "Follow me."

As we walk, the fog on Arraka's map gradually clears, revealing the layout of the corridors behind us, while Fyron leaves no junction unmarked. The silence of the labyrinth presses down on us—sounds deaden within paces, and the black seredium walls swallow every vibration.

"Aren't you going to ask me what the complex symbol I'm marking the walls with means, Barry?" Fyron asks with a grin.

"No, thanks," I say. "It's not worth owing you another favor just to get an answer to that question."

"You are learning fast!" Fyron says, and marks yet another wall with his green symbol.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Arrow, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Arrow)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Avoid
TEXT:

"In that case, I propose we avoid the trail," I say. "Even if we're going to fight the trolls either way, I'd still rather fight them on our own terms, than risk giving them the advantage."

"A valid proposal," Fyron says. "Does anyone have any objections?"

"As I've said, it's all the same to me," Illuna says.

"No objections from me," the tiger says.

Leila remains silent, offering no objections, though she watches the dark blood trail with a slight frown.

"Very well, then," Fyron says. "We will not pursue the blood trail. If we're going to begin exploring the other paths, then our first concern should be the mapping of this maze. Arraka, I understand that you have the ability to generate holograms?"

"Yeah," Arraka says. "But I can't make a map of this place if my magical sense can't pass through the walls."

"It doesn't matter," Fyron says. "You can just draw the map as we walk. What's important is that we don't lose track of where we are. I will be marking the walls periodically, as we advance through the maze, for that same purpose."

"Alright then," Arraka says. "Here goes."

She conjures a glowing miniature projection, similar to the one from the ogre stronghold, where our positions show as colored dots and the surrounding maze is blanketed in black fog, except for our immediate corridor. Meanwhile, Fyron uses his staff to imprint a complex symbol in glowing green ink onto the right-hand wall.

"We're good to go," Fyron says. "Follow me."

As we walk, the fog on Arraka's map gradually clears, revealing the layout of the corridors behind us, while Fyron leaves no junction unmarked. The silence of the labyrinth presses down on us—sounds deaden within paces, and the black seredium walls swallow every vibration.

"Aren't you going to ask me what the complex symbol I'm marking the walls with means, Barry?" Fyron asks with a grin.

"No, thanks," I say. "It's not worth owing you another favor just to get an answer to that question."

"You are learning fast!" Fyron says, and marks yet another wall with his green symbol.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Formation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Formation)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Arrow
TEXT:

#if(v_hearing < 4) {


Minutes later, we reach the end of the blood trail at a dead-end, where a wounded troll sits on the ground with an arrow driven into his chest, leaning his back against the wall. We study the troll and his surroundings from a safe distance, watching for signs of an ambush.

"Are you the... intruders?" the troll asks in Common, breathing heavily.

"We are indeed," Fyron says.

"Please," the troll says, "could you help me... remove this arrow... from my chest?"

"And why would we do something like that?" Fyron says.

"You are... looking for the minotaur... yes?" the troll says. "If you help me... I can lead you to him. I know the way..."

}
#if(v_hearing == 4 && v_ancient_languages < 3) {


As we advance through the corridors, I hear the voices of several trolls talking in low murmurs far ahead.

The language is unfamiliar, so there is no way for me to decipher what they are saying. After exchanging a few sentences, the voices cease, and the corridors fall silent once again.

Minutes later, we reach the end of the blood trail at a dead-end, where a wounded troll sits on the ground with an arrow driven into his chest, leaning his back against the wall. We study the troll and his surroundings from a safe distance, watching for signs of an ambush.

"Are you the... intruders?" the troll asks in Common, breathing heavily.

"We are indeed," Fyron says.

"Please," the troll says, "could you help me... remove this arrow... from my chest?"

"And why would we do something like that?" Fyron says.

"You are... looking for the minotaur... yes?" the troll says. "If you help me... I can lead you to him. I know the way..."

}
#if(v_hearing == 4 && v_ancient_languages >= 3) {


As we advance through the corridors, I hear the voices of several trolls talking in low murmurs far ahead.

The language is an ancient dialect, but the linguistic codices in my stat device allow me to translate their words clearly.

"Please," says one of the trolls. "At least remove the arrow!"

"We'll remove the arrow from your wound as soon as you're done with your task," another troll says. "Now tell me, do you remember what you need to do?"

"Yes," the wounded troll says, breathing heavily. "I need to... lure the intruders... and when they're near me... I press the button."

"Exactly," the other troll says. "And as soon as you press the button, we'll receive our signal, and we'll know that we can ambush the invaders. After we kill them all, you will receive your treatment."

"Yes..." the wounded troll says. "The treatment..."

"Do not fail us like before," the other troll says, and after this last phrase, the corridors fall silent once again.

Minutes later, we reach the end of the blood trail at a dead-end, where a wounded troll sits on the ground with an arrow driven into his chest, leaning his back against the wall. We study the troll and his surroundings from a safe distance, watching for signs of an ambush.

"Are you the... intruders?" the troll asks in Common, breathing heavily.

"We are indeed," Fyron says.

"Please," the troll says, "could you help me... remove this arrow... from my chest?"

"And why would we do something like that?" Fyron says.

"You are... looking for the minotaur... yes?" the troll says. "If you help me... I can lead you to him. I know the way..."

}
choice("Help him remove the arrow", B2-Ch03a-Dead, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Dead) if (v_hearing == 4 || v_hearing < 4)
choice("Leave him to die", B2-Ch03a-Leave, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Leave, v_b2_ch3_troll = 0) if (v_hearing == 4 || v_hearing < 4)
choice(""Where are all the other trolls that you were talking to, earlier?"", B2-Ch03a-Earlier, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Earlier, v_b2_ch3_troll = 1) if (v_hearing == 4 && v_ancient_languages >= 3)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Dead
TEXT:

"You'd better not be lying to us," I say, stepping toward him.

Illuna grabs me by the arm, trying to stop me.

"Just what do you think you're doing?" she says.

"Calm down," I tell her. "I know what I'm doing. Don't you think I can handle one wounded troll by myself?"

Illuna frowns at me, but releases my arm.

"Don't say I didn't warn you," she says.

I approach the troll and kneel beside him to draw the arrow out from his chest.

"Thank you... sir," the troll says. "You are... far too kind..."

As I focus on carefully removing the arrow, the troll shifts in my peripheral vision—gripping something behind his back.

"Barry, get back!" Illuna shouts, but it is too late.

Before I can react, the entire alcove detonates in a blinding explosion, tearing through rock and bone before the sound even registers, and the darkness takes me for good.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Leave
TEXT:

"Looks like a trap to me," I say. "I'm definitely not getting anywhere near that troll. Come on, let's try out some of the other paths."

"I agree," Illuna says. "At this point, I find it highly unlikely for this to be anything other than a trap. Let's get out of here."

"No, wait!" the troll shouts, trying to rise as he coughs dark blood. "I can help you! Don't leave me like this! Come back!"

We ignore the troll's desperate cries for help and turn down a different corridor, while Fyron and Arraka resume their efforts to map the maze.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Formation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Formation)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Earlier
TEXT:

"Where are all the other trolls that you were talking to, earlier?"

The troll panics when he hears my words, trying in vain to look calm and collected.

"W-what other trolls?" the troll says. "I don't know what... you're talking about! I'm the only troll here. The others left me here to... die!"

"You are hiding something behind your back, aren't you?" I tell him. "Some sort of remote? I heard the other trolls telling you to press the button after you convince us to approach you."

"N-no!" the troll says. "You must have... misheard something!"

"You do realize that the remote they gave you is probably meant to trigger an explosion, don't you?" I tell him. "If they really wanted to ambush us, they wouldn't have needed a signal to know when to come. All they had to do was hide somewhere nearby."

"No, that's not true!" the troll shouts. "They would never do something like this!"

"They wouldn't?" I ask him. "What about that arrow in your chest, then? As far as I know, we should be the only intruders in these caverns. Who shot that arrow at you, if not the other trolls?"

"They said they would treat my wound if I... completed my task..." the troll says.

"Hey, if you trust your friends that much, then who am I to convince you otherwise!" I say. "Go ahead and press that button. Your cover has been blown, anyway, so you might as well use that signal to call your friends to help, right?"

The troll looks at me helplessly, holding his thumb over the button, but in the end he lacks the nerve to press it, lowering his head in defeat.

"I see that you've finally decided to stop deluding yourself," I tell him. "That's good. It means that now we might actually be able to work out a deal. How about it? You give me that remote of yours, and I'll remove the arrow for you. Then you can hold your promise of leading us to the minotaur. Sounds good?"

The troll makes no sound for several seconds.

"You speak to me as if I had a choice..." the troll says.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Remote, v_ac_b2_ch3_enemy = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Remote)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Remote
TEXT:

achievement("Once an enemy, now an ally",v_ac_b2_ch3_enemy)
"Well, if you've already made up your mind," I say, "then go ahead and throw that remote over here. I'll make sure to catch it without accidentally pressing the button."

"Take it," the troll says, tossing the remote, and I catch it with both hands. "I throw myself at your mercy..."

"Here, hold this," I tell Illuna, handing her the remote. "Meanwhile, I'll go remove that arrow."

"Keep the remote," Illuna says. "Removing the arrow will fix nothing. He's obviously been poisoned. Otherwise, he would have healed himself by now. Unless I cast a spell to remove the poison, he's not going to get up from there."

She approaches the troll and carefully pulls the poisoned arrow out of his chest. Touching his wound with her hands, a white light gathers between them as the poison is slowly cleansed from the troll's body.

"Your wound is healed," Illuna tells the troll. "Although this could have all been avoided if you had bothered to drink a blasted anti-poison elixir before coming here. Is ten copper pieces really such a high sum to pay for a mercenary?"

"I... didn't think I'd need it..." the troll says.

"Hmph, of course you didn't," Illuna says. "Get up. We've fulfilled our end of the bargain. It's time for you to fulfill yours."

"I will," the troll says, getting to his feet and taking the lead. "Follow me."

"Hold on," I say.

"Oh, what is it now?" Illuna says.

"Hey, troll," I say, lifting the remote in the air. "Watch this!"

I press the button on the remote, and the alcove where the troll was sitting immediately erupts in fire as a loud explosion brings down the walls and ceiling, transforming the dead-end into a heap of smoking rubble.

"I just wanted to make sure that you don't regret your decision of joining us," I tell the troll, looking at the stunned expression on his face.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Illusion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Illusion)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Illusion
TEXT:

"I understand..." the troll says.

"Did you really have to alert every single troll in this maze of our position?" Illuna asks me.

"Think positive!" I say. "The trolls who knew about the trap will now think that we're dead, so they'll be coming here with their guards lowered. All according to plan!"

"Right..." Illuna says.

We follow the troll, and as soon as we reach the end of the corridor, we all instinctively turn right, choosing the only visible path.

"Wait, not that way!" the troll says.

"What do you mean, not this way?" the tiger says. "This way is the only way we can go! Don't tell me that you want us to go back?"

"It's not the only way," the troll says, and walks directly through the solid black wall on the left, disappearing from view.

"This is the correct path," the troll's voice echoes from beyond the stone. "Just walk through that wall. It is an illusion."

"I must admit that I would have never guessed it if you hadn't told me," Fyron says, touching the surface with his hand. "It even mimics the sensation to the touch, to a certain degree. At least, enough to make me not question its authenticity. I wonder how many of these fake walls I've marked on my way here, without even realizing it."

We all walk through the illusion wall, continuing to follow the troll through the maze's sharp twists and turns. As we take another right turn, Arraka's map indicates that we are heading straight into a corridor we already explored.

"Hey, wait up!" Arraka says. "That's not the right way. We've already been through that corridor once. Look at the map!"

"The fake walls are not the only illusions being used in this maze," the troll says. "There is a spell that affects your sense of direction, as well. I see that you've also been marking the walls, aside from drawing the map. I'm sure that you'll believe me when you'll see that the corridor I'm leading you to will be completely devoid of such markings."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Blamed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Blamed)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Blamed
TEXT:

When we reach the corridor, we find out that the troll was not lying. The walls are completely bare of green symbols, even if according to Arraka's map, this should have been a passage we had already visited.

"Damn it!" Arraka snaps. "What a waste of time this map turned out to be."

"The fake walls and the misdirection," the troll says, "are meant to keep intruders inside the maze long enough for our captain to come and finish them off. They're not expecting you to find your way to the minotaur, so his cell will likely only have a few guards posted in front of it. You shouldn't have much trouble freeing the minotaur, but I'm not sure how you're planning to get out alive afterwards."

"Oh, you don't need to worry about that," Fyron says. "We have our escape route covered. We also have room for one more passenger, if you feel like abandoning your post for good."

"I... might just take you up on that offer," the troll says.

"May I ask for what reason the other trolls chose to turn against you?" Fyron asks him.

"I was the one guarding the entrance to the caverns," the troll says. "They blamed me for having let you inside, even though none of you ever went anywhere near the entrance."

"That makes sense," Fyron says, rubbing his chin. "We would never have made it this far undetected, if any of the guards knew about the existence of the secret tunnels. How much further is it until the minotaur?"

"Not much further now," the troll says. "We need to take about three more turns."

When we draw close to our destination, we finally encounter three more trolls posted to guard the minotaur's cell. They barely have time to register our presence before Illuna slices their heads off with her water whips.

"Aren't you going to burn the bodies?" I ask the goblin.

"I'm not wasting any more energy from my staff for something this trivial," Fyron says. "If you want, you can hide the bodies yourself. Not that it would make much of a difference at this point."

choice("Hide the bodies", B2-Ch03a-Hide, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Hide)
choice("Leave the bodies where they are", B2-Ch03a-Minotaur, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Minotaur)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Formation
TEXT:

We navigate the dark labyrinth for fifteen minutes without crossing a single patrol. The seredium corridors warp normal perception—hallways stretch and contract depending on where you look, sharp turns feel strangely disorienting underfoot, and our footsteps scrape against the basalt with a hollow, delayed echo that sounds like someone trailing two paces behind us. Shadows cast by Fyron's glowing orb bend against the walls in unnatural angles. Arraka marks dead-ends on her floating hologram, steering us clear of repeating them, but the dead silence only deepens.

Then the path twists completely.

We round a sweeping curve and halt dead in our tracks. Dead ahead, etched into the jet-black seredium at eye level, burns Fyron's glowing green symbol.

We stare at Arraka's map in disbelief, then back at the marked stone. According to the holographic projection, we are deep in unexplored territory.

"How is this possible?" Illuna says, eyes narrowing at the glowing display. "I've been closely monitoring Arraka's map while she was drawing it, and I am certain that it's accurate. According to the map, this should be an unexplored area. Did the trolls manage to falsify the green symbol?"

"It's not a copy," Fyron says. "My mark cannot be so easily reproduced. And aside from that, I also recognize this corridor. I can tell you with a certainty that we've been here before. Although I'm not exactly sure how we—"

His voice trails off as a certain rock formation in the wall catches his eye. He approaches the rock face and taps it lightly with his staff.

"The goblin is not wrong," a deep voice echoes from the gloom ahead.

Heavy footsteps crunch against the stone as a massive, broad-shouldered troll emerges from the dark. He is encased from neck to boot in anti-magic plating, holding two scimitars in his hands, grinning triumphantly.

"You've been running in circles ever since you entered this maze," the troll says. "I knew that you'd be coming back to this corridor, soon."

"Psst," I whisper to Illuna, shifting behind Leila and the tiger. "Why aren't you killing him?"

"He is wearing anti-magic armor, and he has a helmet," Illuna whispers back to me. "That means that I can't make my water sharp against him, so I won't be able to cut his head off with my water whips."

"Yeah, but isn't anti-magic armor basically just regular armor with some protections on it?" I whisper. "Can't you have Flower burn him alive?"

"There is no anti-magic armor in existence without protection from fire," Illuna whispers. "Casting fire magic on him would be pointless."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Cage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Cage)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Cage
TEXT:

While the two of us are whispering, hiding behind Leila and the tiger, the troll finishes his gloating and prepares to attack.

"There is no way for you to escape," the troll says. "Resign yourselves to your fate. Today is the last day of—"

Leila has had enough. She makes a dash toward the troll, shocking him with her speed, and slashes his head right off with her dagger in an instant.

Instead of dropping to the ground, however, the troll's body attacks her with the two scimitars, which Leila barely manages to avoid by making a quick jump backward.

"That's not how you begin a battle, human," the troll's head says from the ground. "Attacking an enemy out of the blue without giving them proper warning is a mark of cowardice. Did they not teach you proper gladiator etiquette when they brought you to the arena?"

His body then picks up his head from the ground and places it back on top of its neck. Within seconds, the head attaches itself back to the neck, making it look as if there had never been a wound in the first place.

"A troll that doesn't die even when you cut his head off?" Fyron says. "This is not what I signed up for. Illuna, use the cage spell."

"I can only use that spell once per day," Illuna says. "If I use it now—"

"It doesn't matter," Fyron says. "You won't need to use it later. I have a plan. Cage him, now!"

Illuna frowns at him, but does as he asks. She stretches her arm toward the troll, and out of nowhere, a golden cage materializes around him, trapping him inside. It is the same spell that she used to trap us at the ogre fort.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Smoke, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Smoke)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Smoke
TEXT:

"Do you seriously think that a mere cage can stop me?" the troll roars, slashing furiously at the cage's golden bars.

"Yes, we do," Fyron says, and smacks his staff into the ground, generating a cloud of gray smoke that fills the entire corridor.

I hear the sound of rocks grinding to my right, and through the smoke, I see the outlines of my companions moving past me.

"Over here!" Illuna's voice calls, grabbing me by the arm and pulling me toward her.

I follow her through the smoke into a narrow passage leading to the other side of the wall. Once we are all through, Fyron smacks his staff into the cave's wall from the inside, and the opening closes itself back up. He pushes past us to retake the lead, smacking his staff on the other end of the passage to open another secret door, leading us into an unexplored corridor without any markings on the walls.

"That cage isn't going to hold him for long," Illuna says as we all make our way out of the passage, and the secret door closes behind us. "Would you mind telling me what this plan of yours is?"

"He doesn't need to be held for long," Fyron says. "I very much doubt that he knows about the existence of that secret passage, so he's not going to find us anytime soon. More importantly, I recognized that corridor from earlier, and I recognize this one as well. They kept some of the old corridors and included them in the labyrinth when they built this place. If that's the case, then I think I have a pretty good idea of where we are, and the minotaur's cell should not be far from here. Follow me."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Originate, v_ac_b2_ch3_cage = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Originate)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Originate
TEXT:

achievement("The golden cage",v_ac_b2_ch3_cage)
"I've been meaning to ask," I tell Illuna. "Wasn't the golden cage from earlier one of the golden fox's spells? How did you manage to learn it?"

"It's not the fox's spell," Illuna says. "It is a complex spell that can only be used by beings that originate from the magical plane. I learned it from Arraka."

"I see..." I say.

"Hah, I was wondering why I didn't remember there being a wall on this side!" Fyron says.

As we turn to him, we see him walk straight through one of the walls, disappearing from sight.

"Come over here!" Fyron shouts from the other side. "The wall is an illusion. This is how they must have been fooling us for so long. If they've got illusions like this set up, then I wouldn't put it past them to also have a spell cast on this place that messes with our sense of direction. That would explain why Arraka's map didn't help. It doesn't matter, though. If my intuition is correct, we should be almost there."

We all walk through the illusory wall, continuing our journey toward the minotaur. A few minutes later, when we draw close to our destination, we encounter three more trolls posted to guard the minotaur's cell. They barely have time to register our presence before Illuna slices their heads off with her water whips.

"Aren't you going to burn the bodies?" I ask the goblin.

"I'm not wasting any more energy from my staff for something this trivial," Fyron says. "If you want, you can hide the bodies yourself. Not that it would make much of a difference at this point."

choice("Hide the bodies", B2-Ch03a-Hide, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Hide)
choice("Leave the bodies where they are", B2-Ch03a-Minotaur, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Minotaur)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Hide
TEXT:

"Fine, I'll hide them by myself, then," I say.

"Suit yourself," Fyron says. "The minotaur's cell should be a little further down the corridor. You can find us there when you're done."

As the others head toward the minotaur, I prepare to lift one of the troll bodies, when I notice Leila walking to the other end of the corpse and grabbing it by the legs.

"Oh," I say to her, when I understand what she is doing. "Thanks!"

She smiles at me, and together we carry the troll's body over to a nearby secret passage. There's not much we can do about the blood on the floor, but in the dark corridor, it will be harder to notice from a distance than corpses lying in plain sight. Once we finish moving all the bodies and heads into the passage, we close the secret door and hurry to catch up with the others.

Just as we approach them, Fyron uses his staff to cast a spell on a magical panel on the wall. The panel fizzles, unlocking the gate of the cell beside it.

"Perfect," the goblin says, seeing me and Leila approach. "You've arrived just in time!"

With the gate open, the prisoner inside the cell steps outside. He has the head of a bull and the body of a man, taller than Daren and with twice the muscle mass. The minotaur takes a quick glance toward us as he exits, then turns toward the goblin.

"How are you planning to get me out of these caverns?" the minotaur asks.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Passage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Passage)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Minotaur
TEXT:

"Yeah," I say. "I guess there wouldn't be much point in hiding bodies if we're almost at the end of the maze. Let's keep going."

We make our way toward the end of the corridor, where we find a single cell with a magical panel on the wall beside it. Inside the cell, a solitary prisoner with the head of a bull and the body of a man lies on the floor, back propped against the wall. The minotaur is taller than Daren, with twice the muscle mass. When he hears us arrive, he does not move an inch, not even turning his head to look at us.

"Who are you?" the minotaur asks.

"I guess I was right when I figured that they'd place you in the old paralysis cell," Fyron says. "The maze's only purpose was to keep the intruders out, not to keep you in."

"Who are you?" the minotaur asks again.

"I am Fyron, the general of all free goblins in Varathia," Fyron says. "I've come here to recruit you to join my army. What do you say?"

"A goblin army?" the minotaur says.

He laughs bitterly.

"I never thought I'd live to see the day," the minotaur says, "when I would actually seriously consider an offer like this."

"It's not as bad as you might think," Fyron says. "At the very least, in our goblin camp you should have access to more food than here, and you wouldn't have to spend your days locked up in a paralysis cell. Or would you rather spend the rest of your life entertaining these humans?"

"This goblin army of yours..." the minotaur says. "Is it made only of goblins? Or have you also recruited other warriors such as myself to join it before?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Minotaur2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Minotaur2)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Minotaur2
TEXT:

"It is made mostly of goblins," Fyron says, "but our goblin camp accepts all races, as long as they follow our rules, and pay the yearly tribute."

"And how much is the yearly tribute?" the minotaur asks.

"It depends," Fyron says. "On average, it is about one copper piece for each goblin."

"Per year?" the minotaur asks, surprised.

"Per year," Fyron says.

"And why is it that you are recruiting?" the minotaur asks. "Are you preparing for war?"

"That remains to be seen," Fyron says. "For the moment, let's just say that I like being cautious."

The minotaur ponders the goblin's words in silence.

"We can negotiate the terms of your employment later, if you wish," Fyron says. "The last thing I would want is to force you into this, by taking advantage of your current situation. All I want is your word that you will take my offer seriously, and that you will join me on my trip back to the goblin camp. I know how much minotaurs value honor, so as long as you give me your word, that is enough for me. We can discuss the details afterwards."

"You are clearly a skilled diplomat, goblin," the minotaur says. "You have a gift for finding the exact words that someone wants to hear, and you deliver them at just the right moment. Normally, my natural hatred for diplomats would make me not think twice before refusing your offer, but in this case, as you are well aware, I do not have much of a choice."

He takes a breath.

"Very well, general Fyron," the minotaur says. "I give you my word. As long as you free me from here, I will accompany you to your goblin camp, and I will take your employment offer as seriously as I am able."

"That is all I wanted to hear," Fyron says with a grin.

He uses his staff to cast a spell on the panel outside the cell, deactivating the paralysis field and swinging the gate open.

"How are you planning to get me out of these caverns?" the minotaur asks as he rises from the floor and walks out of his cell.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Passage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Passage)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Passage
TEXT:

"By using magic," Fyron says. "It just so happens that Illuna and I are both rather adept at spell casting, and we both have the ability to teleport, within certain limitations."

He turns to Illuna.

"Illuna," Fyron says. "Can you teleport the minotaur to the surface? As far as I know, your daily teleportation spell does not have a weight limit. Mine, on the other hand costs additional energy for every extra pound. I would rather you take the minotaur with you, since he is by far the heaviest of the group. I will be with you shortly, after I lead your friends to the secret passage I mentioned earlier."

Illuna nods, and the goblin turns toward the minotaur.

"Once you are on the surface, Illuna won't be able to help you," Fyron says. "Everyone in this city knows your face from the arena, so she can't be seen fighting alongside you, unless she wants the city guard hunting her across Thilias. Don't wait for us. As soon as you are out of the caverns, make a rush towards the city gates, smash right through them, and don't stop until you reach the Mertram woods. We will meet you there."

"I will be waiting," the minotaur says.

"We should get going," Illuna says, taking the minotaur's hand to prepare for teleportation.

She gives us all one last look, pausing when her eyes meet mine.

"Don't die," she tells me, simply.

"I won't," I say.

She casts her teleportation spell, and both she and the minotaur vanish from sight.

"This way!" Fyron says, heading back down the corridor.

He halts before a wall, tapping it three times with his staff. A secret door grinds open, revealing a hidden passage.

"This tunnel will lead you back to the upper levels," Fyron says. "I trust that you will be able to find your way back to your cells from there."

"We'll manage," I say.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch03a-Unique, v_current_scene = B2-Ch03a-Unique)


ID: B2-Ch03a-Unique
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch3_troll == 0 && v_ch5_goblins != 1) {
"I think I should also mention that the tunnel can only be opened from this side," Fyron says. "Once you enter it, you won't be able to go back."

The goblin turns to Leila.

"As far as I know," he says, "Barry is required to return to his cell, unless he wants to get disqualified from the tournament. You, however, are bound by no such rules. If you want, I could give you a lift. There is enough energy in my staff remaining to teleport one extra person."

Thank you, Leila writes, but I'm fine. I will take the tunnel.

"Are you sure?" the goblin asks. "I promise that I won't charge you... much."

I'm positive, Leila writes.

"Very well, then," Fyron says, readying his staff and signaling the tiger to come near him.

"I wish you both luck," the goblin says. "Next time we meet, I hope it will be under better circumstances."

He casts his spell, vanishing into thin air alongside the tiger. Leila and I waste no time, stepping into the secret tunnel as the door shuts itself behind us.

You have some very... unique friends, Leila writes, smiling as we walk through the dark corridor.

}
#if(v_b2_ch3_troll == 1 && v_ch5_goblins != 1) {
"I think I should also mention that the tunnel can only be opened from this side," Fyron says. "Once you enter it, you won't be able to go back."

The goblin turns to Leila.

"As far as I know," he says, "Barry is required to return to his cell, unless he wants to get disqualified from the tournament. You, however, are bound by no such rules. If you want, I could give you a lift. There is enough energy in my staff remaining to teleport one extra person."

Thank you, Leila writes, but I'm fine. I will take the tunnel.

"Are you sure?" the goblin asks. "I promise that I won't charge you... much."

I'm positive, Leila writes.

"Very well, then," Fyron says, readying his staff and signaling the tiger and the troll to come near him.

"I wish you both luck," the goblin says. "Next time we meet, I hope it will be under better circumstances."

He casts his spell, vanishing into thin air alongside the tiger and the troll. Leila and I waste no time, stepping into the secret tunnel as the door shuts itself behind us.

You have some very... unique friends, Leila writes, smiling as we walk through the dark corridor.

}
#if(v_b2_ch3_troll == 0 && v_ch5_goblins == 1) {
"I think I should also mention that the tunnel can only be opened from this side," Fyron says. "Once you enter it, you won't be able to go back."

The goblin turns to Leila.

"As far as I know," he says, "Barry is required to return to his cell, unless he wants to get disqualified from the tournament. You, however, are bound by no such rules. If you want, I could give you a lift. There is enough energy in my staff remaining to teleport one extra person."

Thank you, Leila writes, but I'm fine. I will take the tunnel.

"Are you sure?" the goblin asks. "I promise that I won't charge you... much."

I'm positive, Leila writes.

"Very well, then," Fyron says, readying his staff and signaling the tiger to come near him.

"Oh, there was one last thing I wanted to tell you," Fyron says, looking toward me. "A few days ago, four goblins that I'd exiled from my camp came back, with the two copper pieces that they required in order to pay the yearly tribute. When I asked them where they got the money from, they said that they got it from a scrawny human, who was travelling with a dark-skinned warrior dressed in heavy armor, and with two other women. They also said that the warrior had an 'X' shaped scar on his forehead. Since the warrior they were talking about is clearly the healer in armor, I can only assume that the scrawny human was you?"

"Yeah, that was me," I say.

"Hmm..." Fyron says, studying me with a curious look for a few seconds. "Well, the four goblins said that they were very eager to meet you again, so if you ever find your way into our goblin camp, be sure to drop by their house. I'm certain that they will be of help to you in one way or another."

"Sure thing," I say. "If I ever have business there, I will pay them a visit."

Fyron nods, preparing to cast his teleportation spell.

"I wish you both luck," the goblin says. "Next time we meet, I hope it will be under better circumstances."

He casts his spell, vanishing into thin air alongside the tiger. Leila and I waste no time, stepping into the secret tunnel as the door shuts itself behind us.

You have some very... unique friends, Leila writes, smiling as we walk through the dark corridor.

}
#if(v_b2_ch3_troll == 1 && v_ch5_goblins == 1) {
"I think I should also mention that the tunnel can only be opened from this side," Fyron says. "Once you enter it, you won't be able to go back."

The goblin turns to Leila.

"As far as I know," he says, "Barry is required to return to his cell, unless he wants to get disqualified from the tournament. You, however, are bound by no such rules. If you want, I could give you a lift. There is enough energy in my staff remaining to teleport one extra person."

Thank you, Leila writes, but I'm fine. I will take the tunnel.

"Are you sure?" the goblin asks. "I promise that I won't charge you... much."

I'm positive, Leila writes.

"Very well, then," Fyron says, readying his staff and signaling the tiger and the troll to come near him.

"Oh, there was one last thing I wanted to tell you," Fyron says, looking toward me. "A few days ago, four goblins that I'd exiled from my camp came back, with the two copper pieces that they required in order to pay the yearly tribute. When I asked them where they got the money from, they said that they got it from a scrawny human, who was travelling with a dark-skinned warrior dressed in heavy armor, and with two other women. They also said that the warrior had an 'X' shaped scar on his forehead. Since the warrior they were talking about is clearly the healer in armor, I can only assume that the scrawny human was you?"

